;; soul-refined — the agent formulates a network; Lain compiles it.
;; DESIGN DRAFT, not runnable yet. Adapter names below are proposed contracts.
;; Reuses the existing CPS compiler/session infrastructure, not a new evaluator.
;; soul.lain remains unchanged. No model/weather calls have been executed.

;; The experiment is a persistent relationship, not a sequence of turns:
;; the environment's connected description feeds the model; the model's source
;; extends that same environment through the existing CPS compiler. Relevant
;; changes propagate back through the description without a host refresh loop.
;; Read alongside propagators/doc/how-to-think-in-lain.md, especially 4, 9, 19–22.

;; Compositional operators such as |> require their existing library.
(load-lain "propagators/compiler_2/runtime/lain/combinator.lain")

;; 1. Preserve your environment-extension vocabulary.
;; These surface names still need mapping to existing environment primitives.
;; Definitions and lexical captures should use compiler-native bindings.
;; String concept names follow soul.lain; name-to-binding mapping is still an
;; adapter contract, not an assumption that host quote syntax exists in Lain.
(define construct-concept-of
  (network [idea content]
    (network [env]
      (extend-env env (env:slot idea content)))))

;; 2. Generic runtime: two concurrently maintained relationships.
;; The request is information introduced into the environment by the caller,
;; so it participates in the same live description as capabilities and results.
;; These lines declare topology; their textual order does not schedule work.
(define runtime
  (network [environment model]
    (let [e (lexical-child environment)]
      (-> (describe-environment e) (inputs-of model))
      (incremental-compile (actions-of model) e)
      (list e model))))

;; describe-environment is a connected transformation, not a one-time dump.
;; It maintains the model-visible view of relevant bindings, definitions,
;; observations and diagnostics, preserving their dependencies and support.
;; Late information and relevant environment extensions update that view.
;; An unrelated unknown field must not block information already available.
;; New bindings must be discoverable through a live environment interface;
;; retaining a frozen map of binding names would not satisfy this contract.
;; Relevance is part of the view's dependencies, not an ad hoc refresh decision.
;; Do not flatten live compound accessors or open lists into frozen host values
;; inside this relationship, or substitute a one-time topology trace for it.

;; inputs-of exposes the model's connected input interface. Related updates
;; arrive automatically through the relationship above. The adapter determines
;; when that evolving information supports an inference and what happens when
;; inputs change during inference. Sampling, accumulation or cancellation, if
;; needed, must have explicit temporal semantics at that boundary.
;; A provider may need serialized input for one inference; that snapshot belongs
;; at its effect boundary and must not sever the upstream live relationship.

;; actions-of exposes generated Lain expressions as supported publications.
;; incremental-compile maintains their connection to the evolving environment
;; using existing CPS/session source extension. Each accepted source publication
;; has identity/support; repropagating it must not install duplicate topology.
;; Compiler diagnostics and results become observable environment information,
;; so the same description relationship carries them back to the model.
;; These are proposed surface contracts, not already-verified installed operators.
;; No separate application dispatcher or explicit model/tool feedback loop.

;; This cycle needs understood temporal/support semantics, not an assumption
;; that arbitrary model feedback converges. Use existing versioned publication
;; and TMS mechanisms for replacement/retraction; incompatible plain writes to
;; one cell are contradictions, not successive messages or source revisions.
;; Completion and resource bounds belong to the model/effect relationship.

;; 3. A minimal seed environment; tools and providers are supplied extensions.
;; compile-source is the host bridge backed by the existing CPS/session APIs.
;; publish-message is an effectful capability, not an implicit host side channel.
(define default-environment
  (network [compile-source publish-message]
    (|> (empty-env)
        (construct-concept-of "incremental-compile" compile-source)
        (construct-concept-of "message-to-user" publish-message))))

;; 4. Weather-specific capabilities extend that same environment.
;; weather-at accepts a place, absolute date and timezone, and returns an
;; observation with source, observed-at and validity support.
;; umbrella-advice is a supplied pure function over a weather observation.
;; The generic runtime does not know these names or their workflow.
(define weather-environment
  (network [base-env weather-at umbrella-advice]
    (|> base-env
        lexical-child
        (construct-concept-of "weather-at" weather-at)
        (construct-concept-of "umbrella-advice" umbrella-advice))))

;; 5. EXAMPLE OF AGENT-GENERATED SOURCE, not a hardcoded harness procedure.
;; Given "Will I need an umbrella tomorrow in Amsterdam?", the model should
;; formulate a parameterized composition like this in its invocation env:
(define umbrella-for
  (network [place date timezone]
    (let [forecast (weather-at place date timezone)]
      (umbrella-advice forecast))))

;; Then emit an application connected to the response capability:
;; (-> (umbrella-for "Amsterdam" requested-date requested-timezone)
;;     (message-to-user user))
;; requested-date/timezone/user are explicit request-context bindings.
;; Resolving "tomorrow" depends on the request timestamp and timezone.
;; A second request can compile a new application of umbrella-for for another
;; place, without generating its body again. It may still need a fresh forecast.
;; If observations reveal missing information, the model extends the network.
;; Reuse the definition; do not mistake reuse for caching all external results.

;; 6. Concrete existing implementation anchors (Clojure namespaces).
;; propagators.compiler-2.api/compile-source [source environment options]
;;   returns :network, :environment, :installed-propagator-ids,
;;   :application-metadata, :result-cell and :diagnostics.
;; propagators.compiler-2.cps-core
;;   assembles CPS handlers for definitions, networks, applications and scope.
;; propagators.compiler-2.session
;;   provides immutable sessions, session-extension, extend-session and advance.
;; propagators.compiler-2.session.host/extend-source!
;;   provides source extension at the existing host boundary.
;; Use these existing layers; do not call both compilation paths for one revision.
;; The immutable extension API rejects conflicting monotone declarations;
;; replacement/removal requires explicit version/lifecycle handling, not a claim
;; that an arbitrary existing binding can be overwritten in place.

;; 7. Dependencies and effects belong to the existing computational machinery.
;; Reuse compiler binding/application structure and TMS support propagation.
;; Verify lexical captures, supplied arguments and external observation support
;; survive composition; do not assume metadata alone proves complete provenance.
;; Session Dependency capabilities and TMS justifications are related integration
;; points, not interchangeable concepts. Adapters must attach appropriate support.
;; Model evaluation, weather IO and message publication use existing effect
;; boundaries/receipts. No duplicate scheduler or tool-dispatch interpreter.
;; Compiling/admitting a source extension can lead to execution: do not treat
;; source installation as a harmless preview or promise rollback of sent effects.
;; The host source API prepares an atomic extension; retain that admission path.
;; Do not substitute the legacy per-form loader, which can retain a prefix
;; on failure. Atomic admission still cannot undo effects dispatched afterward.

;; 8. Small implementation plan and observable success criteria.
;; A. Establish the live environment/model/compiler relationships once.
;;    Publish a request into the environment; verify it reaches model inputs.
;;    Publish a related observation or add a visible capability afterward;
;;    verify its description propagates without rerunning runtime or refreshing.
;;    Leave an unrelated field unresolved; available information must still flow.
;;    Withdraw supported information; verify the model-visible view follows it.
;; B. Install weather and response capabilities through session extensions.
;; C. Ask the model to GENERATE umbrella-for; inspect the resulting network,
;;    its applications, captured bindings, support and result in existing views.
;; D. Ask for another place; compile an application referencing that definition.
;;    Verify the definition is reused and the weather request has new inputs.
;; E. Change a supported input; verify dependent pure results update and external
;;    effects follow explicit request/refresh semantics rather than accidental replay.
;; F. Save source revisions plus identity/provenance/receipts; reopen for inspection
;;    without IO. Durable reconstruction is work to verify, not a property inferred
;;    merely from using immutable in-memory compiler sessions.
;;
;; Core MVP question: can the model formulate a useful reusable computational
;; network for the request? Provider choice and semantic memory search are secondary.
